> On Mar 2, 2017, at 6:36 PM, Tobias Konitzer <tobias....@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>>
>> Thanks for all the answers so far! I never liked putting group hierarchies on the cut-points, that never seemed logical to me. So I have gone back and put the hierarchies on the linear predictor, roughly modeled after page 143 in stan reference 2.14 and gelman/hill 2007, chapter 13 (model attached).
>>
>> The idea is the following: We have a group-specific vector of random effects, that we draw from a multivariate normal, modeling the across-group-within-random-effects variations and correlations in the Varcov matrix of this multivariate normal. Then, we draw class of outcome from an ordered logit where the combination of group-specific random effects is the linear predictor.
>>
>> But, the model does not converge at all. Is there something obvious that I am missing?
>>
>> Thanks again,
>> Tobi
>> On Sunday, February 26, 2017 at 5:39:32 PM UTC-8, Andrew Gelman wrote:
>> Hey, I think I wrote that!
>>
>> > On Feb 26, 2017, at 6:51 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>> >
>> > I also realized we did say something on our priors page.  I should look
>> > at our own Wiki:
>> >
>> > https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/wiki/Prior-Choice-Recommendations
>> >
>> >
>> > • Prior for cutpoints in ordered logit or probit regression
>> >  • For full Bayes, uniform priors typically should be ok, I think.
>> >  • For modal estimation, put in some pseudodata in each category to prevent "cutpoint collapse."
>> >  • Cutpoints are ordered (by definition). Put the prior on the differences between the cutpoints rather than the cutpoints themselves.
>> >  • Alternatively, put a prior on the cutpoints and partially pool them, not to a constant, but to a linear function. That is, if the cutpoints are c[1],...,c[K], don't do c[k] ~ normal(mu, sigma); instead do c[k] ~ normal(a + b*k, sigma), estimating the hyperparameters a and b from data. It will probably make sense to put informative priors on a, b, and sigma too.
>> >
