I was trying to perform something like chi-square test from stan. The results are some what different. What should I do to compare two binomial distributions ?

data {
        int N1;
        int n1;
        int N2;
        int n2;
}
parameters{
        real theta1;
        real theta2;
}
model{
        n1 ~ binomial(N1, theta1);
        n2 ~ binomial(N2, theta2);
}

This is my R code

n1 <- 37
N1 <- 370
n2 <- 65
N2 <- 851
fit1 <-  stan("like_chi-square.stan", data = c("n1","N1", "n2", "N2"), chains = 4, iter = 4000, warmup = 1000)
df <- data.frame(th1 = extract(fit1)$theta1, th2 = extract(fit1)$theta2)
ggplot(df)+
        geom_histogram(aes(x = th1), col = "red", fill = NA)+
        geom_histogram(aes(x = th2),col = "blue", fill = NA)
mean(extract(fit1)$theta1 < extract(fit1)$theta2)

this gives .08

but the chi-square p value is .2

m <- matrix(c(37, 333, 65, 786), ncol = 2)
chisq.test(m)

Did I do it wrongly ? or this difference is just due to the simulation ?

From udae01c57a3c54a3a at groups.google.com  Wed Mar  8 22:17:12 2017
From: udae01c57a3c54a3a at groups.google.com (Mike Lawrence)
Date: Wed, 08 Mar 2017 22:17:12 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Using stan to compare two groups in a contingency table like Chi-square test
Message-ID: <fmxK_P71xmM.tYJPrY7gAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <fmxK_P71xmM.ICgrOgXfAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <fmxK_P71xmM.ICgrOgXfAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/fmxK_P71xmM/m/tYJPrY7gAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When doing Bayesian inference, you want to look at the full distribution of the posterior, not just the mean (which is *not* analogous to a p-value in any event).

So you should do:

    summary(mean(extract(fit1)$theta1 < extract(fit1)$theta2))

and maybe

    hist(mean(extract(fit1)$theta1 < extract(fit1)$theta2))

If you're specifically interested in the hypothesis of zero difference between the probabilities, then you can see where zero falls relative to this distribution. If most of the mass is far away from zero, you can probably discount zero as a credible hypothesis.

--
Mike Lawrence
Graduate Student
Department of Psychology & Neuroscience
Dalhousie University

~ Certainty is (possibly) folly ~

