Hi,

On Monday, March 13, 2017 at 3:11:50 AM UTC+2, Nick Menzies wrote:
> Hi All -- I wanted to provide an update on this effort. I implemented Rob's code (thanks Rob!) and it has mostly been good, the one major challenge has been that I couldn't achieve good sampling -- Rhat always high, n-eff very low. I suspect that this is linked to the large region in the grid where I have no observations.

I expected this could happen. You should probably use inducing point approach (in spatial or spectral space). These require a bit more equations, but not impossible to implement (I can send some links later, but don't have time now).

> As a side note, are there any approaches to GPs based on taxi-cab distance rather than euclidian distance? My reason for asking is that this would greatly reduce the number of discrete distances involved, which I assume could speed the matrix calculations.

Yes, but you need to be careful as not all covariance functions combined with taxi-cab distance produce positive-semi-definite covariance matrix. And I don't think you would get a speed advantage.

