You need to set the chain ids or you'll get the same
results if you start with the same seed.  Were the draws
in the chains all the same?

- Bob

> On Mar 24, 2017, at 11:23 AM, Ru Wang <wangr...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> My dataset is 14454*38 and my stan is very slow to run, and i thought if I decrease the number of chain=, then the time may drop, that is why i set chian=1 for each time. I tried for 8 times, however, the result is still bothering in two respects:
> 1: it took almost 5 days for each run(8 runs with chain=1,iteration=5000 for each time). That means i just submit the code for 8 times at the same time.
> 2. I got 8 totally same sample chains.
> And the attachments are my codes. Can you tell me what I should do?
> Will the number of chian=  affect the time for each run? The computer in lab at my school has 12 processors. Thanks, Ben.
>
> On Fri, Mar 24, 2017 at 10:07 AM, Ben Goodrich <goodri...@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Friday, March 24, 2017 at 11:00:33 AM UTC-4, Ru Wang wrote:
> So in this sentence "algorithm = c("NUTS", "HMC", "Fixed_param")" , HMC means (static) HMC, and both (static) HMC and NUTS are implementations of HMC?
>
> Yes. It also means that dynamic HMC (NUTS) is the default.
>
> I one more question. If I set chain=4 in one run time, I don't need to set seed values and I can still get four different sample chains. However, if I want to run for 4 times and each time with chain=1, then I need to set seed values for each time to get different sample chains?
>
> You can and should set the seed to be the same thing each time but also set the chain_id to be 1, 2, 3, or 4. Then the results will be different. But honestly there is no reason to not just run it once with chains = 4.
>
> Ben
>
