On Tue, Mar 28, 2017 at 07:33:59AM -0400, Joshua N Pritikin wrote:
> Symmetric is easy. How do I ensure that it is positive definite? I'm not
> sure that I can easily specify priors on the parameters (like in section
> 10.2 of the manual) to ensure that it is positive definite. Can I use an
> lkj or wishart prior?

For example, if I compute my covariance matrix Sigma as usual,

    FA[embedD+1] = 1 + eta[px] * L3;
    FA[embedD+2] = L2 + zeta[px] * L3;
    FA[embedD+3] = L3;
    Sigma = quad_form_sym(S, FA);

Then I could add a prior for the correlation aspect,

    stddev = sqrt(diagonal(Sigma));
    for (cx in 1:embedD) {
      for (rx in 1:embedD) {
        if (rx == cx) continue;
        cor[rx,cx] = Sigma[rx,cx] / (stddev[rx] * stddev[cx]);
      }
    }
    cor ~ lkj_corr(1);

However, when I tried this, the parser astutely complains,

  Left-hand side of sampling statement (~) may contain a non-linear transform of a parameter or local variable.
  If it does, you need to include a target += statement with the log absolute determinant of the Jacobian of the transform.

This is approach feasible? How do I obtain the jacobian adjustment?

--
